message.content:
On the lateral chest X-ray, there is blunting and increased opacity in the posterior costophrenic sulcus. In a standard lateral projection with the "L" marker indicating the patient's left side (posteriorly), the posterior sulcus corresponds to the left hemithorax. The right posterior sulcus is typically more anterior or obscured by the diaphragm contour in this view; the visible fluid layering here localizes to the left.

Uncertainty: Low for left-sided localization given the marker and typical lateral anatomy, though small effusions can be subtle.

{"answer":"B. left pleural effusion","reason":"Posterior costophrenic sulcus blunting on the lateral view with L marker indicates left-sided fluid."}

